Only used by the container/xorriso FALLBACK path
(tasks/prepare_with_container.yml). When proxmox-auto-install-assistant
itself runs (the preferred path), it writes this file inside the ISO
itself and this template is never rendered.

VERIFIED 2026-08-09 against real vendor output. This is no longer a
reconstruction: proxmox-auto-install-assistant 9.2.8 was run with
`prepare-iso --fetch-from iso` against a Proxmox VE 9.2-1 ISO, and the
resulting /auto-installer-mode.toml was extracted and read verbatim. It
is exactly:

    mode = "iso"
    partition_label = "proxmox-ais"

    [http]

The empty [http] table is present even in iso mode -- the vendor
serialises the whole settings struct, so the section header appears with
no keys under it. Reproduced here for byte-fidelity with the vendor
tool; the installer does not require the section to be populated when
mode is "iso".

Why `mode` matters, learned the hard way: a stock ISO's grub.cfg only
tests that this file EXISTS (`if [ -f auto-installer-mode.toml ]`) to
enable both the boot timeout and the "Install Proxmox VE (Automated)"
menu entry. It never parses the contents. But the running installer
DOES read `mode` to decide where to fetch answer.toml from. So an
unrecognised mode value produces a very misleading failure: the ISO
boots, the menu times out into the automated entry, the installer
starts, silently falls back to the INTERACTIVE installer, and then sits
at its licence prompt forever. From the outside that looks identical to
booting a completely unprepared ISO -- same media read pattern, same
byte count -- so it is easy to misdiagnose as a disk-selection or
answer-file-placement problem when it is neither. Do not guess at this
value; if a future Proxmox release changes it, re-derive it by running
the vendor tool and reading its output, as was done here.
-#}
mode = "iso"
partition_label = "{{ asmb8_autoinstall_iso_partition_label | default('proxmox-ais') }}"

[http]
